Description: spicyR tests whether the spatial co-localisation of pairs of cell types
    differs between groups of patients, or is associated with patient outcome, in
    cell-resolution spatial omics data (imaging mass cytometry, CODEX, MIBI, Xenium,
    CosMx and others). Its default method, spicyR Cell, counts the cells of one type
    within a radius of each cell of another and compares the count with its exact
    expectation under random labelling of the observed cells. Images are combined
    within patients by a frailty generalised estimating equation, and differences are
    tested with a small-sample cluster-robust (CR2) variance with patients as the
    units. Covariates, several conditions, several radii and survival outcomes are
    supported, and the original image-level test is kept as an option. The
    computations run in a C++ core shared with the Python package spicyr.
